  1001	For each $i\in [c]$, if  $w\in V(F_i)$, then let
  1002	$F_i^w$ be a spanning tree of $H^w$; if  $w\notin V(F_i)$, then let $F_i^w$ be the empty graph.
  1003	\end{itemize}
  1004	For
  1005	$i\in [c]$, let $E_i=E(F_i)\cup \bigcup_{w\in V(G_{s+1})}E(F_i^w)$ and let
  1006	$T_i=G_s[E_i]$. It is obvious that $T_1,\ldots,T_c$ are internally disjoint
  1007	$U^s$-Steiner trees. We need to ensure that each labelled vertex
  1008	of $G^s$ satisfies ($\star$).
  1009	
  1010	In fact, without loss of generality, we only need to choose an arbitrary labelled vertex $u\in G_{s+1}$ and prove that each vertex of $W^u$ satisfies ($\star$).
  1011	This is because $F_i^w$ is clear when $w$ is an unlabelled vertex (in the case $F_i^w$ is either a spanning tree of $H^w$ or the empty graph).
  1012	By Eq. (\ref{eq-root}) and Fact \ref{fact-lian}, $|W^u|\leq k-1$ since $u\neq v_{root}$.
  1013	
  1014	Since $d_{F_i}(u)\leq 2$ for each $i\in [c]$ and $u\in U^s$, it follows that $|E_{u,i}|=|V_{u,i}|\leq 2$.
  1015	Therefore, we can divide $F_1,\ldots,F_c$ into the following five types on $u$:
  1016	
  1017	{\bf Type 1}: $|V_{u,i}|=1$ and $V_{u,i}\subseteq W^u$.
  1018	
  1019	{\bf Type 2}: $|V_{u,i}|=1$ and $V_{u,i}\nsubseteq W^u$.
  1020	
  1021	{\bf Type 3}: $|V_{u,i}|=2$ and $V_{u,i}\subseteq W^u$.
  1022	
  1023	{\bf Type 4}: $|V_{u,i}|=2$ and $V_{u,i}\cap W^u=\emptyset$.
  1024	
  1025	{\bf Type 5}: $|V_{u,i}|=2$ and $|V_{u,i}\cap W^u|=1$.
  1026	
  1027	If $F_i$ is a graph of Type $j \ (1\leq j\leq 5)$ on $u$, then we also call $F_i^u$ a {\em
  1028	$W^u$-Steiner tree of Type $j$}.
  1029	%We call the vertex in $V_{u,i}\cap W^u$ a {\em special vertex} if $F_i^u$ is a graph of type 1 or 5, see Figure \ref{Type-Method}.
  1030	Suppose there are $n_j(u)$ trees $F_i$ that belong to Type $j$ on $u$ for $j\in [5]$. Then
  1031	\begin{align}\label{n1u-n5u}
  1032	\sum_{i=1}^{5}n_j(u)=c.
  1033	\end{align}
  1034	It is obvious that $n_j(v_{root})=0$ for each $j\in [5]$.
  1035	Let
  1036	$$
  1037	R(u)=V(H^u)-W^u-\bigcup_{i\in[c]}V_{u,i}.
  1038	$$
  1039	Note that $R(u)$ is the set of unlabelled vertices in $G_s$, which will be used to construct the $W^u$-Steiner trees in $H^u$.
  1040	It is clear that
  1041	\begin{equation}\label{R(u)}
  1042	|R(u)|=\ell-|W^u|-[n_2(u)+2n_4(u)+n_5(u)].
  1043	\end{equation}
  1044	
  1045	\begin{figure}[h]
  1046	    \centering
  1047	    \includegraphics[width=420pt]{Type-Method1.eps}\\
  1048	    \caption{Five types and methods 1, 1a, 2 and 3.} \label{Type-Method}
  1049	\end{figure}
  1050	
  1051	
  1052	We have the following five methods to choose $F_1^u,\ldots,F_c^u$ for the labelled vertex $u\in G_{s+1}$ (see Figure \ref{Type-Method}).
  1053	
  1054	{\bf Method 1} If $F_i$ is a graph of Type 1 and $|W^u|\geq 2$, or $F_i$ is a graph of Type 3, then let $F^u_i=x\vee W^u$, where $x\in R(u)$.
  1055	
  1056	{\bf Method 1a} If $F_i$ is a graph of Type 1 and $|W^u|\geq 2$, then let $F^u_i$ be a Hamiltonian path of $H^u[W^u]$ such that the vertex in $V_{u,i}$ is an endpoint of this Hamiltonian path; if $F_i$ is a graph of Type 3, then let $F^u_i$ be a Hamiltonian path of $H^u[W^u]$ such that the endpoints of $F_i$ are two vertices in $V_{u,i}$.
  1057	
  1058	
  1059	{\bf Method 2} If  $F_i$ is a graph of Type 2 or Type 5, say $x$ is the only vertex of $V_{u,i}$ with $x\notin W^u$, then let $F^u_i=x\vee W^u$.
  1060	
  1061	
  1062	{\bf Method 3} If $F_i$ is a graph of Type 4, say $V_{u,i}=\{x,y\}$, then let $F^u_i=xy\cup (x\vee W^u)$.
  1063	
  1064	{\bf Method 4} If $F_i$ is a graph of Type 1 and $|W^u|=1$, then let $F_i^u$ be the empty graph.
  1065	
  1066	It is worth noting that the method is deterministic if $F_i^u$ is a graph of Types 2, 4 and 5, or $F_i^u$ is a graph of Type 1 and $|W^u|=1$.
  1067	So we firstly construct these $F_i^u$s by using Methods 2, 3 and 4, respectively, and then construct $F^u_i$s of Type 1 with $|W^u|\geq 2$ and construct $F^u_i$s of Type 3 by using Method 1 or Method 1a.
  1068	
  1069	The following is an algorithm constructing $c$ internally disjoint $U$-Steiner trees of $G$.
  1070	
  1071	\smallskip
  1072	
  1073	\begin{algorithm}[H]
  1074	\footnotesize
  1075	\caption{The construction of $c$ internally disjoint $U$-Steiner trees of $G$}%Ëã·šÃû×Ö
  1076	\label{algorithm:Caculate Stain tree}
  1077	\LinesNumbered %ÒªÇóÏÔÊŸÐÐºÅ
  1078	\KwIn{$U$, $c$ and $G=S(n,\ell)$ with $|W^{root}|\geq 2$}% ÊäÈë²ÎÊý
  1079	\KwOut{$c$ internally disjoint $U$-Steiner trees $T'_1,T'_2,\ldots, T'_c$ of $G$}% Êä³ö
  1080	$T'_1,T'_2,\ldots,T'_c$ are empty graphs\;%\;ÓÃÓÚ»»ÐÐ
  1081	$s=n$ //(* in initial step, $G^n$ is a single vertex*)\;
  1082	\For{$s\geq 1$}{
  1083	\For{each vertex $u$ of $G_s$}{
  1084	 \If{ $u$ is an unlabelled vertex}{
  1085	   \For{ $1\leq i\leq c$}{
  1086	      \eIf{ $u$ is a vertex of $F_i$}{
  1087	                   $F_i^u$ is a spanning tree of $H^u$\;}{
  1088	                  $\ F_i^u$ is is the empty graph;}
  1089			        $T'_i=T'_i\cup F_i^u$\;
  1090	                 $i=i+1$\;
  1091	                          }
  1092	    }
  1093	
  1094	
  1095	 \If{ $u$ is a labelled vertex}{
  1096	  $\mu=|R(u)|$\;
  1097	   \For{ $1\leq i\leq c$}{
  1098	
  1099	
  1100	       \If{ $T'_i$ is of Type 2 or Type 5}{
  1101	                    construct $F_i^u$ by using Method 2\;
  1102	                 }
  1103	
  1104	       \If{ $T'_i$ is of Type 4}{
  1105	       construct $F_i^u$ by using Method 3\;
  1106	                                }
  1107	
  1108	       \If{ $T'_i$ is of Type 1 and $|W^u|=1$}{
  1109	       construct $F_i^u$ by using Method 4\;
  1110	                                              }
  1111	
  1112	        \If{ either $T'_i$ is of Type 1 and $|W^u|\geq 2$, or $T'_i$ is of Type 3}{
  1113	           \If{ $\mu>0$}{
  1114	           construct $F_i^u$ by using Method 1\;
  1115	           $\mu=\mu-1$\; }
  1116	
  1117	            \If{ $\mu\leq 0$}{
  1118	             construct $F_i^u$ by using Method 1a\;             }
  1119	                                              }
  1120	
  1121	
  1122			        $T'_i=T'_i\cup F_i^u$\;
  1123	                $i=i+1$\;
  1124	                          }
  1125	    }
  1126	
  1127	
  1128	
  1129	 }
  1130	 $s=s-1$\;
  1131	}
  1132	\end{algorithm}
  1133	
  1134	
  1135	
  1136	Algorithm \ref{algorithm:Caculate Stain tree} is an algorithm for finding $c$ internally disjoint $U$-Steiner trees of $G$.
  1137	For each step of $s$, the algorithm will construct $c$ internally disjoint $U^{s-1}$-steiner trees $T'_1,T'_2,\ldots,T'_c$.
  1138	For convenience, we denote each $T_i'$ by $T_i^s$ after the $s$ step of outer ``for'', that is, $T_1^s,T_2^s,\ldots,T_c^s$ are $c$ internally disjoint $U^s$-Steiner trees in $G^s$ generated in Algorithm \ref{algorithm:Caculate Stain tree}.
  1139	If Algorithm \ref{algorithm:Caculate Stain tree} is correct, then Lines 16--40 indicate that $d_{T'_i}(x)\leq 2$ for any $x\in W^u$, and hence ($\star$) always holds.
  1140	
  1141	We now check the correctness of the  algorithm.
  1142	Since $F_i^u$ is deterministic when $u$ is an unlabelled vertex (Lines 5--15 of Algorithm \ref{algorithm:Caculate Stain tree}), and $F_i^u$ is deterministic if $u$ is a labelled vertex and either $F_i^u$ is a graph of Types 2, 4 and 5, or $F_i^u$ is a graph of Type 1 and $|W^u|=1$ (Lines 19--27 of Algorithm \ref{algorithm:Caculate Stain tree}), we only need to talk about the labelled vertex $u$ with $|W^u|\geq 2$ and  check the correctness of Lines 28--36 of Algorithm \ref{algorithm:Caculate Stain tree}. That is, to ensure that there exist $n_1(u)$ $F_i^u$s of Type 1 and $n_3(u)$ $F_i^u$s of Type 3.
  1143	If $F_i^u$ is constructed by Method 1, then $F_i^u$ is a star with center in $R(u)$ (say the center of the star is $a_i$); if $F_i^u$ is constructed by Method 1a, then $F_i^u$ is a Hamiltonian path of $H^u$ with endpoints in $V_{u,i}$.
