   302	\subsection{Sierpi\'{n}ski graphs}
   303	
   304	
   305	
   306	In 1997, Klav\v{z}ar and Milutinovi\'{c} introduced the
   307	concept of Sierpi\'{n}ski graph $S(n,\ell)$ in \cite{S.Klavzar}.
   308	We denote $n$-tuples $V^{n}$ by the set
   309	$$V^{n}=\{\langle
   310	u_0u_1\cdots u_{n-1}\rangle|\ u_i \in \{0,1,\ldots,\ell-1\} \textrm{ and } i\in \{0,1,\ldots,n-1\}\}.$$
   311	 A {\em word} $u$ of size $n$ are denoted by $\langle
   312	u_0u_1,\cdots,u_{n-1}\rangle$ in which $u_i \in
   313	\{0,\ldots,\ell-1\}$.
   314	The concatenation of two words $u=\langle u_0u_1\cdots u_{n-1}
   315	\rangle$ and $v=\langle v_0v_1\cdots v_{n-1} \rangle$ is denoted by
   316	$uv$.
   317	
   318	
   319	\begin{definition}
   320	The Sierpi\'{n}ski graph $S(n, \ell)$ is defined as below, for
   321	$n\geq 1$ and $\ell \geq 3$, the vertex set of $S(n, \ell)$ consists
   322	of all $n$-tuples of integers $0,1,\cdots,\ell-1$. That is,
   323	$V(S(n,\ell))=V^n$, where $uv=(u_0u_1\cdots u_{n-1},v_0v_1\cdots v_{n-1} )$ is an edge of $E(S(n,\ell))$
   324	if and only if there exists $d\in \{0,1,\ldots,\ell-1\}$ such that:
   325	$(1)$ $u_j = v_j$, if $j < d$; $(2)$ $u_d \neq v_d$; $(3)$ $u_j =
   326	v_d$ and $v_j = u_d$, if $j >d$.
   327	\end{definition}
   328	
   329	\begin{figure}[h]
   330	    \centering
   331	    \includegraphics[width=180pt]{S24.eps}\\
   332	    \caption{$S(2,4)$} \label{S(2,4)}
   333	\end{figure}
   334	
   335	Sierpi\'{n}ski graph $S(2,4)$ is shown in Figure \ref{S(2,4)}.
   336	Note that $S(n,\ell)$ can be constructed recursively as follows:
   337	$S(1,\ell)$ is isomorphic to $K_{\ell}$, which vertex set is 1-tuples set $\{0,\ldots,\ell-1\}$. To
   338	construct $S(n,\ell)$ for $n > 1$, we take copies of $\ell$ times $S(n-1,\ell)$
   339	and add the
   340	letter $i$ on the top of the vertices in $i$-th copy of $S(n-1,\ell)$, denoted by $S^{i}(n, \ell)$.
   341	Note that there is exactly one edge (\emph{bridge edge}) between $S^{i}(n, \ell)$ and $S^{j}(n, \ell)$, $i\neq j$, namely the edge between vertices $\langle ij\cdots j \rangle$ and $\langle ji\cdots i\rangle$.
   342	
   343	
   344	
   345	The vertices $(\underbrace{i,i,\cdots,i}_{n})$, $i\in \{0,1,\ldots,\ell-1\}$
   346	are the \emph{extreme vertices} of $S(n, \ell)$.
   347	Note that an extreme vertex $u$ of $S(n,\ell)$ has degree $d(u)=\ell-1$.
   348	For $i\in \{0,\ldots,\ell-1\}$ and $n \geq 2$, let $S^{i}(n-1,\ell)$ denote the
   349	subgraph of $S(n,\ell)$ induced by the vertices of the form
   350	$\{\langle iu_1\cdots u_{n-1}\rangle \,|\,0\leq
   351	u_i \leq \ell-1\}$.
   352	The vertex set $V(S(n,\ell))$ can be partitioned
   353	into $\ell$ parts $V(S^{0}(n-1,\ell)),
   354	V(S^{1}(n-1,\ell)),\ldots,V(S^{\ell-1}(n-1,\ell))$.
   355	For each $0 \leq i \leq \ell-1$, $S^{i}(n-1,\ell)$ is isomorphic to
   356	$S(n-1,\ell)$. Note that $V(S(n,\ell))=V(S^{0}(n-1,\ell))\cup \cdots \cup V(S^{\ell-1}(n-1,\ell))$ and $S(n,\ell)$ is the graph obtained from $S^{0}(n-1,\ell), \ldots ,
   357	S^{\ell-1}(n-1,\ell)$ by adding exactly one edge (\emph{bridge edge}) between $S^{i}(n-1, \ell)$ and $S^{j}(n-1, \ell)$, $i\neq j$, and the bridge edge joins $\langle ij\cdots j \rangle$ and  $ \langle ji\cdots i\rangle$ (notices that $\langle ij\cdots j \rangle$ and  $ \langle ji\cdots i\rangle$ are extreme vertices of $S^{i}(n-1, \ell)$ and $S^{j}(n-1, \ell)$, respectively, if we regard $S^{i}(n-1, \ell)$ and $S^{j}(n-1, \ell)$ as two copies of $S(n-1,\ell)$).
   358	
   359	
   360	
   361	
   362	
   363	Sierpi\'{n}ski graphs generalize Hanoi graphs which can be viewed as ``discrete'' finite versions of a Sierpi\'nski gasket \cite{Sahu2020, Gu2022}.
   364	Xue considered the Hamiltionicity
   365	and path $t$-coloring of Sierpi\'{n}ski-like graphs in \cite{Xue};
   366	furthermore, they proved that $Val(S(n,k))=Val(S[n,k])=\lfloor
   367	k/2\rfloor$, where $Val(S(n,k))$ is the linear arboricity of
   368	Sierpi\'{n}ski graphs. We remark that although Sierpi\'{n}ski graphs are not regular, they are ``almost'' regular as the extreme vertices have degrees one less than the degrees of non-extreme vertices.
   369	
   370	
   371	
   372	
   373	
   374	
   375	
   376	
   377	\subsection{Preliminaries and our results}
   378	
   379	
   380	Chartrand et al. \cite{Chartrand} and Li et al. \cite{LMS} obtained
   381	the exact value of $\kappa_k(K_n)$.
   382	
   383	\begin{theorem}[\cite{LMS,Chartrand}]\label{Gary Chartrand etl.}
   384	For every two integers $n$ and $k$ with $2\leq k\leq n$,
   385	$$
   386	\kappa_k(K_n)=\lambda_{k}(K_n)=n-\lceil k/2 \rceil.
   387	$$
   388	\end{theorem}
   389	
   390	
   391	The following result is on the Hamiltonian decomposition of Sierpi\'{n}ski graphs.
   392	\begin{theorem}{\upshape\cite{Xue}}
   393	\label{Hamitondecomposed1}
   394	$(1)$ For even $\ell \geq 2$, $S(n, \ell)$ can be decomposed into edge disjoint union of $\frac{\ell}{2}$ Hamiltonian paths of which the
   395	end vertices are extreme vertices.
   396	
   397	$(2)$ For odd $\ell \geq 3$, there exist $\frac{\ell-1}{2}$ edge-disjoint Hamiltonian paths whose two end vertices are extreme
   398	vertices in $S(n, \ell)$.
   399	\end{theorem}
   400	In fact, Theorem \ref{Hamitondecomposed1}
   401	is used in the proof of Theorem \ref{main},
   402	which give an lower bound for
   403	the generalized $k$-edge connectivity
   404	of Sierpi\'{n}ski graphs $S(n, \ell)$.
   405	We require the following result.
   406	
   407	\begin{theorem}[\cite{B.L.chen,XueZUO}]\label{Hamiton-complete}
   408	Suppose that $G$ is a complete graph with $V(G)=\{v_0,\cdots,v_{N-1}\}$. If $N=2n$, then $G$ can be decomposed into $n$ Hamiltonian paths
   409	$$
   410	\{i\in \{1,2,\ldots,n\}:\ L_i=v_{0+i}v_{1+i}v_{2n-1+i}v_{2+i}v_{2n-2+i}\cdots v_{n+1+i}v_{n+1}\},
   411	$$
   412	where the subscripts take modulo $2n$.
   413	If $N=2n+1$, then $G$ can be decomposed into $n$ Hamiltonian paths
   414	$$
   415	\{i\in \{1,2,\ldots,n\}:\ L_i=\ v_{0+i}v_{1+i}v_{2n+i}v_{2+i}v_{2n-1+i}\cdots v_{n+i}v_{n+1+i}\}
   416	$$
   417	and a matching $M=\{v_{n-i}v_{n+1}:\ i\in \{1,2,\ldots,n\}\}$, where the subscripts take modulo $2n+1$.
   418	\end{theorem}
   419	The following result is derived
   420	from Theorem \ref{Hamiton-complete}, and we will use it later.
   421	\begin{corollary}\label{s-Hamil-path}
   422	Let $s$ be an integer with $s\leq \frac{N}{2}$. Suppose that $G$ is the complete graph with $V(G)=\{v_1,\cdots,v_N\}$ and $\mathcal{S}=\{\ \{v_{i_1},v_{i_2}\}:i\in\{1,2,\ldots,s\}\}$ is a collection of pairwise disjoint $2$-subsets of $V(G)$.
   423	Then there are $s$ edge-disjoint Hamiltonian paths $L_1,\cdots,L_s$ such that $v_{i_1},v_{i_2}$ are endpoints of $L_i$.
   424	\end{corollary}
   425	
   426	Our main result is  as follows.
   427	\begin{theorem}\label{main}
   428	$(i)$ For $3\leq k\leq \ell$, we have $$\kappa_{k}(S(n,\ell))=
   429	\lambda_{k}(S(n,\ell))=\ell - \left\lceil k/2 \right\rceil.$$
   430	
   431	$(ii)$ For $\ell+1 \leq k \leq {\ell}^n$, we have
   432	$$\kappa_{k}(S(n,\ell))\leq \left\lfloor \ell/2 \right\rfloor\mbox{ and }
   433	\lambda_{k}(S(n,\ell))=\left\lfloor \ell/2 \right\rfloor.$$
   434	\end{theorem}
   435	
   436	
   437	
   438	
   439	
   440	
   441	\section{Proof of Theorem \ref{main}}\label{section-main}
   442	
   443	This section is organized as follows.
   444	We first introduce some notations, operations and auxiliary graphs that are used in the proof. Then we give a transitional theorem (Theorem \ref{Theorem_lower_bond}), which is the main tool for the proof of Theorem \ref{main}.  Theorem \ref{Theorem_lower_bond} will be proved by constructing an algorithm, and the proof takes up almost entire Section \ref{section-main} (the proof is divided into two parts:  the construction of algorithm and the proof of its correctness). 
   445	Finally, we complete the proof of  Theorem \ref{main} by using Theorem \ref{Theorem_lower_bond}.
   446	 
   447	\subsection{Notations, operations and auxiliary graphs} 
   448	 
   449	For $1\leq s<n$, by the definition of the Sierpi\'{n}ski graph $S(n,\ell)$, we have that $G=S(n,\ell)$ consists of $\ell^{n-s}$ Sierpi\'{n}ski graphs $S(s,\ell)$, and we call each such $S(s,\ell)$ an {\em $s$-atom} of $G$.
   450	Note that $\mathcal{P}=\{V(H):\ H \mbox{ is an } s\mbox{-atom of } G\}$ is a partition of $V(G)$.
   451	We use $G_s$ to denote the graph $G/\mathcal{P}$.
   452	It is obvious that $G_s$ is a simple graph and $G_s=S(n-s,\ell)$.
   453	Note that $G_n$ is a single vertex and $G_0=G$.
   454	For a vertex $u$ of $G_s$, we use $A_{s,u}$ to denote the $s$-atom of $G$ which is contracted into $u$.
   455	See Figures \ref{$G=G_0$},  \ref{$G=G_1$} and  \ref{$G=G_2$} for illustrations.
   456	
   457	\begin{table}[!htbp]
   458	\centering
   459	\begin{tabular}{c|p{11cm}}
   460	\hline
   461	  {\bf Notation} & {\bf Corresponding interpretation} \\
   462	 \hline
   463	 $s$-atom &  A copy of $S(s,\ell)$  in $G=S(n,\ell)$, where $0\leq s\leq n$.        \\
   464	 \hline
   465	 $G_s$ & The graph obtained from $G$ by contracting all $\ell^{n-s}$  $s$-atoms in $G$.       \\
   466	 \hline
   467	  $A_{u,s}$  &  The $s$-atom in $G$ which is contracted into $u$, where $u\in V(G_s)$.    \\
